<CoursePage title="Leaving is a skill." description={courseMeta.description} eyebrow="THE COURSE / SLOP UNIVERSITY">
 <div class="guide-grid"><article class="prose"><h2>Who this is for</h2><p>You use apps, memberships or free trials, and want a reliable way to decide what to keep and how to leave. You need a browser and a way to take notes. You do not need to code or know consumer-law terminology.</p><h2>What you will be able to do</h2><ol><li>Identify recurring services, their billers and the purchasing accounts.</li><li>Read payment frequency, commitment and cancellation conditions separately.</li><li>Find a relevant official route and recognise when its conditions do not match your purchase.</li><li>Verify the result, preserve a useful record and explain an unresolved problem.</li></ol><h2>How the weeks fit together</h2><p>The first three weeks build the method. Five case lessons apply it to Apple, Google Play, Adobe and Netflix. The final four weeks cover offers, confirmation and support, before an unfamiliar-case practical.</p><p>A lecture introduces the question. A workshop turns it into an annotated record or an explanation. Three assessments gather those outputs into an inventory, a field guide and a completed case.</p><h2>The point of view</h2><p>Being able to leave is part of being able to choose. Speed is useful, but a fast click on the wrong plan is not success. We ask whether the result matches your intention and whether you can show that it happened.</p><p>Examples have dates and sources. A historical dispute, a current help article and a fictional teaching screen serve different jobs, and are labelled accordingly.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button button-dark" href={withBase("/lectures/")}>Start with week 1 <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="text-link" href={withBase("/assessments/")}>See the assessments</a></div></article><aside class="course-factbox"><span class="micro">YOUR COURSE AT A GLANCE</span><dl><dt>Course</dt><dd>{courseMeta.code}</dd><dt>Session</dt><dd>{courseMeta.session} {courseMeta.year}</dd><dt>Teaching</dt><dd>27 July – 30 October</dd><dt>Weekly rhythm</dt><dd>Lecture + Wednesday workshop</dd><dt>Independent work</dt><dd>About 4 hours total per week</dd><dt>Prerequisites</dt><dd>None. Bring a browser.</dd><dt>Practice accounts</dt><dd>Always fictional.</dd></dl><a class="text-link" href={withBase("/policies/")}>Policies & support ↗</a></aside></div>
</CoursePage>
